/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Technology (batch z) */
WB.L("barcodes-qr-codes", "technology", "beginner", "Barcodes and QR codes: reading the stripes",
  "Lines drawn in beach sand, a pack of chewing gum in Ohio and a Japanese board game all helped create the codes you scan every day.",
  [
    ["Lines in the sand", "The barcode began as an idea sketched in beach sand in 1948, but took decades to become practical.",
      "In 1948 the head of an American food chain reportedly asked a Drexel Institute dean whether checkouts could be automated. Graduate student Bernard Silver overheard and told his friend Norman Joseph Woodland. Woodland recalled the breakthrough on a Miami beach. Thinking of the Morse code he learned as a Boy Scout, he dragged his fingers through the sand, turning dots and dashes into thin and thick lines.\n\nThe pair filed a patent in 1949, granted in 1952, including a circular 'bullseye' version readable from any direction. But nothing could read it cheaply. Practical barcodes had to wait more than two decades for lasers and microchips."],
    ["A pack of gum", "The 1974 scan of a chewing gum pack launched the barcode age, which paid off once enough products carried codes.",
      "In the early 1970s American grocers agreed on one standard, the Universal Product Code, or UPC. The winning design came from IBM, where Woodland then worked. It used straight vertical bars, since the bullseye tended to smudge on fast printing presses.\n\nOn 26 June 1974, at a Marsh supermarket in Troy, Ohio, a cashier scanned a ten-pack of Wrigley's Juicy Fruit gum. Take-up was slow, because shops needed expensive scanners and manufacturers had to print codes on everything. Once enough products carried them, the benefits snowballed: faster queues, fewer pricing errors and detailed data on what sold and when. Europe adopted a compatible 13-digit system, which Australia uses too."],
    ["Reading the stripes", "A scanner reads the pattern of light and dark bars as a product number, and the shop's computer looks up the price.",
      "A barcode is a message written in light and shadow. The scanner shines light across the stripes, traditionally a red laser, though many modern scanners use a small camera. Black bars absorb light and white spaces reflect it, so a sensor sees a rapidly changing pattern. Each digit is a particular combination of bar and space widths. Guard patterns at the start, middle and end show where the code begins and whether it is being read upside down.\n\nThe number rarely contains the price. It identifies the product, and the shop's computer looks up the current price in its database. That is why a price change does not mean relabelling every item."],
    ["A code in two dimensions", "Denso Wave's QR code stores far more than a barcode by using a grid, with finder squares to spot it quickly.",
      "An ordinary barcode holds only a short number, and Japanese car factories scanned many for every box of parts. At Denso Wave, then part of the car-parts maker Denso, engineer Masahiro Hara led a team to design something better. He has said the idea came while playing Go, whose black and white stones on a grid suggested storing information in two dimensions.\n\nReleased in 1994, the Quick Response code could hold thousands of characters, including Japanese kanji. The hardest puzzle was making it instantly recognisable among printed clutter. The team studied printed material to find a shape that almost never appears by chance, and settled on the nested squares now in three corners of every QR code."],
    ["Tough, free and risky", "QR codes survive damage thanks to error correction, but each one is a link you cannot read, so check before trusting it.",
      "The three large squares are finder patterns. Wherever the code sits in an image, the scanner spots them and works out which way is up. The rest of the grid holds data plus error correction, based on mathematics published by Irving Reed and Gustave Solomon in 1960, also used on CDs and in spacecraft communication. At the highest of four protection levels, roughly 30 per cent of the code can be dirty or covered and still be recovered. That is why a logo can sit over the middle of a code.\n\nDenso Wave pledged not to enforce its patent, so anyone can use QR codes freely. Scammers exploit that, sticking fake codes over real ones. Check the web address before entering anything."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did the first barcode patent not lead to scanners straight away?", ["Shops refused to print any kind of code on products","The bullseye design could only be read from one direction","Cheap readers needed lasers and microchips, not yet built","Grocers had already agreed on a different standard"], 2, "The idea was sound, but practical scanners needed laser and microchip technology that came decades later."],
    [1, "Why did barcodes spread slowly after the first scan in 1974?", ["The code could not be read by early scanners at all","Customers disliked having prices removed from items","Printing presses could not produce straight bars","Scanners were costly and products needed codes printed"], 3, "Benefits snowballed only once enough products carried codes to justify the scanners."],
    [2, "What does the number in a barcode usually identify?", ["The current price of the item","The shop where the item was made","The date the item was packed","The product itself, not its price"], 3, "Prices live in the shop's database, so they can change without relabelling products."],
    [3, "Why was the QR code developed for car factories?", ["Barcodes could not be printed on car parts","Workers needed to store more data than one short barcode number","Factories wanted codes that could be read only by machines","Workers needed codes that could be read in the dark"], 1, "Storing data in two dimensions lets a single code hold thousands of characters."],
    [4, "Why can a QR code still scan with a logo printed over its middle?", ["The scanner only reads the three corner squares","Logos are printed in an ink the camera ignores","Built-in error correction can recover the hidden data","The data is repeated in every corner square"], 2, "Extra error-correction data lets up to about 30 per cent of a code be damaged and still read."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A pack of chewing gum made history in 1974. Find out how stripes learned to carry data."});

WB.L("y2k-bug", "technology", "beginner", "Y2K: the disaster that didn't happen",
  "As 1999 drew to a close, the world braced for computers to think it was 1900. Midnight came and almost nothing broke. Was it all hype, or one of history's great quiet successes?",
  [
    ["Saving two digits", "Programmers wrote years as two digits to save scarce memory, leaving old software that might read 2000 as 1900.",
      "In the 1960s and 1970s computer memory and storage were astonishingly expensive, and a standard punch card had only 80 columns. Programmers saved space by writing years as two digits: 68 instead of 1968. Few expected the programs to last decades, yet many did. Banking, payroll, airline and government systems were built in layers on old code, often in COBOL, and rarely replaced.\n\nBy the 1990s vast amounts of software assumed every year began with 19. When the calendar ticked over to 2000, the year 00 might be read as 1900. The problem became known as Y2K, short for Year 2000."],
    ["Arithmetic, not explosions", "The danger was date arithmetic going wrong in many connected systems at once, including hidden embedded chips.",
      "The fear was never that computers would explode. It was arithmetic. If a system works out age by subtracting birth year from the current year, 00 minus 65 gives minus 65. Interest, card expiry dates, warranties and record deletion all rely on comparing dates, and a sorted list might put 2000 before 1999. Some programs also failed to treat 2000 as a leap year, because century years are leap years only if divisible by 400.\n\nTiny processors sit in lifts, power stations and medical devices, and nobody had a full list of which tracked dates. Most did not, but organisations could not know without testing. The worry was many failures at once, in connected systems."],
    ["The great repair job", "Armies of programmers spent years fixing old code, and the cost is often estimated in the hundreds of billions of dollars.",
      "Through the late 1990s, programmers combed through old code line by line, and retired COBOL experts were in demand again. Common fixes were expanding years to four digits, or 'windowing', where a program treats two-digit years below a cut-off, say 50, as 20xx and the rest as 19xx.\n\nWorldwide cost estimates vary widely, often falling between 300 and 600 billion US dollars. Australia, like many countries, ran national awareness and readiness programs, and governments set up command centres to monitor the changeover. Some critics said much of the money went to overcautious consultants. Others noted that the work also cleaned up and documented ageing systems that needed attention anyway."],
    ["Midnight arrives", "The changeover brought only minor glitches, which left many people asking whether the warnings had been hype.",
      "Australia and the Pacific reached midnight hours before Europe and North America, so the world watched them for early signs of trouble. None came on a large scale. There were glitches. Some websites showed the year as 19100, because programs added 1900 to the year, and a few ticketing and payment machines misbehaved. In Japan some monitoring equipment at nuclear plants reported errors, without any danger to safety. A US military satellite intelligence system had several hours of problems.\n\nMost faults were fixed quickly and passed almost unnoticed. Within days, some newspapers that had warned of chaos were asking whether it had all been a beat-up."],
    ["The preparedness paradox", "When prevention works, the absence of disaster can make the warning look foolish, and time bugs still lurk.",
      "Y2K is a classic case of the preparedness paradox: fix a problem before it bites, and the lack of disaster can make the warning look silly. Experts still debate how bad things would have been. Some countries that spent little also had few problems, partly because they relied less on old systems. Yet many who did the work insist they found serious faults that would have caused real damage.\n\nDate bugs have not gone away. Many systems count seconds since 1 January 1970, and where that count is stored as a 32-bit number it will overflow on 19 January 2038. Much software has been updated, but the next deadline is ticking."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did programmers write years with only two digits?", ["Memory and storage were expensive, so they saved space","Four-digit years could not be stored in any language","Early computers could not count past 99","Punch cards had no room for numbers"], 0, "Saving two digits per date added up when storage was scarce, and nobody expected the code to last."],
    [1, "What was the real danger of Y2K?", ["Computers physically failing at midnight","Wrong date calculations in many linked systems","Clocks stopping on every device","Software deleting itself deliberately"], 1, "Wrong date arithmetic, in many systems at once, was the concern rather than anything exploding."],
    [2, "What does 'windowing' do to fix Y2K?", ["Treats two-digit years below a cut-off as 20xx","Replaces every old computer with a newer model","Stores each year as a word instead of digits","Blocks dates after 1999 from being entered"], 0, "Windowing was a quick repair that avoided rewriting every date field to four digits."],
    [3, "What happened when midnight arrived in 2000?", ["Banks and power grids failed around the world","Only minor glitches occurred and most were fixed quickly","Nothing at all went wrong anywhere","Air travel was shut down for a week"], 1, "Small faults appeared, but there was no large-scale breakdown."],
    [4, "What is the preparedness paradox?", ["Preparing for a problem always makes it worse","Successful prevention can make the warning look unnecessary","Warnings are always exaggerated by the media","Problems solved early are always more expensive"], 1, "When prevention works, nothing happens, which can make the effort seem like hype."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"The world feared computers would think it was 1900. Why did almost nothing happen?"});

WB.L("search-engines", "technology", "beginner", "How a search engine finds anything",
  "Type a few words and, in a fraction of a second, the right page appears out of billions. It is not searching the internet when you press Enter. It did that long before.",
  [
    ["No catalogue", "The early web outgrew hand-built directories and simple word matching, so search needed a smarter way to judge pages.",
      "When the World Wide Web opened to the public in the early 1990s there were only a handful of websites, and people kept lists. Within a few years there were millions of pages, written by anyone and linked every which way, with no central catalogue.\n\nEarly helpers took two approaches. Yahoo began in 1994 as a hand-built directory, with people sorting sites into categories like a library. Others, such as AltaVista, used software to read pages automatically and match the words you typed. Directories could not keep up with the web's growth, and word-matching was easily fooled: a page repeating a word hundreds of times could jump to the top. Search needed a better way to judge which pages were worth showing."],
    ["Crawlers and the index", "Crawlers read the web ahead of time and build an index, so your search looks up a prepared list rather than scanning the internet.",
      "Behind every search engine is a crawler, also called a spider or bot. It starts with known web addresses, downloads each page, notes every link and adds the new addresses to its list. This reaches much of the public web, but never all of it: pages behind logins are left out, and a small file called robots.txt, a convention from 1994, lets sites ask crawlers to stay away.\n\nA search engine does not scan the internet when you press Enter. It has already built an index, like the one in a textbook, listing for each word the pages that contain it. Searching 'koala habitat' finds pages on both lists. This inverted index runs to well over a hundred million gigabytes, so answers return in a fraction of a second."],
    ["Links as votes", "PageRank treated links as citations, so a page linked by many important pages ranked higher.",
      "In 1996 two Stanford PhD students, Larry Page and Sergey Brin, began a research project nicknamed BackRub. Their insight came from academic publishing, where an important paper is one that many others cite. They treated a link from one page to another as a citation. A page linked by many others was probably useful, and a link from an important page counted for more than one from an obscure page.\n\nThey called the measure PageRank, a pun on Page's surname. Their company, launched in 1998 as Google, took its name from 'googol', the number 1 followed by 100 zeros, with a change of spelling. Its results were noticeably better than rivals', and within a few years it dominated web search."],
    ["Ranking today", "Modern ranking weighs many signals, including meaning, freshness and your location, not just links.",
      "PageRank still matters, but it is now one ingredient among many. Search engines try to work out what you meant, not just what you typed, so a search for 'how tall is Uluru' can surface a page saying 'Uluru rises 348 metres above the plain'. They also consider how recent a page is, whether it works on phones, your language and where you are, which is why 'pizza near me' gives different answers in Perth and Hobart.\n\nMachine learning increasingly helps interpret meaning. How signals are combined is a closely guarded secret, partly for competitive reasons and partly to stop people gaming the system. A whole industry, search engine optimisation, exists to help sites climb the rankings."],
    ["Who pays for free", "Search is free because advertisers pay for clicks, so it pays to tell ads from results.",
      "Running a search engine costs a fortune, yet you pay nothing. The money comes mainly from advertising. When you search for 'running shoes', advertisers compete in an automated auction, run in a split second, for the chance to show you a link, and they typically pay only when someone clicks. A search is a powerful moment for advertisers, because you are telling the engine exactly what you want right now.\n\nThis model explains a few things. Sponsored results often appear above ordinary ones, marked with a small label. Engines also learn from what people click. Notice the difference between an ad and a result, and remember the top link is not automatically the best one."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did simple word-matching search fail on the early web?", ["Pages could repeat a word to jump to the top","Software could not read pages automatically","The web had too few pages to search","Directories were too slow to build by hand"], 0, "Word counts were easy to game, so search needed a better way to judge quality."],
    [1, "Why do search results appear so quickly?", ["The engine scans the internet live when you press Enter","A prebuilt index lists which pages contain each word","Websites send their pages to you directly","Results are saved from the last person who searched"], 1, "The crawling happens in advance, so your search is just a fast lookup in an index."],
    [2, "What was the key idea behind PageRank?", ["Pages with the most repeated words should rank highest","Links from important pages should count for more","Pages should be ranked purely by their newest content","Pages with the shortest addresses should rank first"], 1, "Treating links like citations let quality, not repetition, drive ranking."],
    [3, "Why can 'pizza near me' give different results in Perth and Hobart?", ["Search engines consider where you are searching from","Perth has more pizza shops than any other city","The engine ignores what you typed","Results are chosen at random for each city"], 0, "Modern ranking uses many signals, including location, language and meaning."],
    [4, "How do search engines mainly earn money?", ["Charging users a monthly fee","Selling the pages they have crawled","Advertisers paying, usually per click","Taking a share of every purchase you make"], 2, "Ads are bought in a split-second auction, so sponsored results can appear above ordinary ones."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"It does not search the internet when you press Enter. Here is what it does instead."});

WB.L("mobile-networks", "technology", "beginner", "How a mobile call finds you",
  "Your phone is a radio, quietly chatting with towers you never notice. Here is how a call reaches you, even on a moving train.",
  [
    ["A two-way radio", "Your phone talks by radio only to the nearest base station, and the rest of the call travels by cable and fibre.",
      "When you call a friend across the street, your phones do not talk directly. Your phone sends radio signals to the nearest base station, usually antennas on a tower, rooftop or pole. From there the call travels over cables and optical fibre through the operator's network, then back out through the base station near your friend. Radio is only the first and last link.\n\nPhones transmit at low power to save battery, so base stations must be spread across the landscape, often only a few hundred metres apart in busy cities. The central problem for engineers was letting millions of people share a limited range of radio frequencies at once without drowning each other out."],
    ["Cells and handover", "Dividing an area into small cells lets frequencies be reused, and handover keeps your call going as you move.",
      "The answer, proposed at Bell Labs in 1947 by Douglas Ring and W. Rae Young, was to divide an area into small zones called cells, each with its own base station. Neighbouring cells use different frequencies, but distant cells can reuse the same ones. Planners draw hexagon honeycombs, though real cells are messy shapes bent by hills and buildings. Capacity grows as cells shrink, which is why cities have many small ones. Hence 'cellular', and why Americans say cell phones.\n\nCalls survive moving between cells through handover. Your phone constantly measures nearby signals, and the network switches your call to the new cell in a fraction of a second. With no neighbouring cell, as on a remote highway, the call drops."],
    ["What the SIM does", "A SIM proves who you are by answering a secret-key challenge, which stops others from cloning your phone.",
      "The SIM card, or its built-in digital version, the eSIM, is a tiny secure computer. It stores an identity number linked to your account and a secret key designed never to leave the chip. When your phone connects, the network sends a random challenge. The SIM combines it with the secret key to produce an answer, and the network, holding a copy of the key, checks the result. A match lets you in.\n\nThis lets the network bill the right person and makes it hard to impersonate someone else. Older analogue phones had no such protection: calls could be overheard with a radio scanner, and identities cloned to run up bills on another person's account. Even idle, your phone checks in so the network knows roughly where you are."],
    ["From 1G to 5G", "Each mobile generation added something new, from digital voice and texting to video, and 5G needs many more small cells.",
      "1G arrived in the 1980s, carrying voice as analogue radio signals. Australia's first cellular network opened in 1987. 2G, in the early 1990s, went digital, improving security and making room for text messages. The first text was sent on 3 December 1992, when engineer Neil Papworth typed 'Merry Christmas' from a computer to a colleague's phone in Britain. Messages were capped at 160 characters, a limit that shaped how a generation wrote.\n\n3G, from the early 2000s, made mobile internet practical, and 4G, from around 2010, made video streaming and apps routine. 5G adds higher speeds and shorter delays, partly by using higher frequencies with a shorter range, which means many more small cells."],
    ["Old networks switch off", "Old networks are switched off to reuse their frequencies, and 000 works from any mobile.",
      "Radio spectrum is limited, so operators periodically switch off old networks to reuse their frequencies. Australia closed its analogue network around 2000, its 2G networks during the 2010s, and its 3G networks during 2024. Some older phones that relied on 3G for voice calls suddenly could not call anyone, including emergency services. If you or an older relative are unsure whether a phone still works properly, your provider can check it.\n\nIn an emergency, call 000 from any mobile. If your own network has no coverage but another does, your emergency call can be carried on that other network. The number 112 also works from mobiles, but 000 remains Australia's main emergency number."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is the main job of the radio link in a mobile call?", ["Carrying the whole call from phone to phone","Connecting your phone to the nearest base station","Storing your call until the other person answers","Sending the call directly to your friend's phone"], 1, "Radio covers only the first and last link, with the rest carried over cable and fibre."],
    [1, "Why are mobile networks divided into cells?", ["So the same frequencies can be reused by distant cells","So each phone can have its own private frequency","So signals travel further than a single tower allows","So hills and buildings stop interfering with signals"], 0, "Reusing frequencies in cells far apart lets millions share limited spectrum."],
    [2, "How does a SIM prove your identity to the network?", ["It sends its secret key straight to the network","It combines a random challenge with a secret key","It displays your phone number to the tower","It uses your voice to confirm who you are"], 1, "The key never leaves the chip, so the network can check the answer without exposing the secret."],
    [3, "Why does 5G need many more small cells?", ["Its higher frequencies travel shorter distances","It carries only voice calls instead of data","It needs a separate tower for every phone","It reuses analogue signals from 1G"], 0, "Higher frequencies give more speed but cover less distance."],
    [4, "What can you do if your own network has no coverage in an emergency?", ["Wait until your signal returns","Call 000, which can be carried on another network","Send a text message to a family member","Call your provider to switch networks"], 1, "A 000 call can use any available network, so it can work even without your own coverage."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Your phone is quietly chatting with towers you never notice. Here is how a call finds you."});

WB.L("how-screens-work", "technology", "beginner", "How screens make pictures",
  "Everything on your screen, from a sunset photo to this sentence, is built from millions of tiny lights in just three colours.",
  [
    ["Dots and three colours", "Every screen is a grid of pixels, each mixing red, green and blue light to make any colour.",
      "Look at a screen through a magnifying glass, or a drop of water, and the illusion falls apart. The image is really a grid of tiny dots called pixels. A 4K television has 3840 pixels across and 2160 down, more than eight million in all. A modern phone packs in several hundred per inch, so the dots are invisible at normal distance.\n\nEach pixel is usually made of three subpixels: red, green and blue. Varying their brightness produces millions of colours. Red and green together look yellow, all three at full strength look white, and all off look black. This is additive mixing, the opposite of paint. It works because the retina has three types of cone cells, and a screen simply stimulates them in the right proportions."],
    ["LCD: a light shutter", "An LCD shines a constant backlight through filters and liquid crystals that twist to let more or less light through.",
      "Most monitors and many televisions use liquid crystal displays, or LCDs. Behind the panel is a backlight, today usually LEDs, that shines white light the whole time. In front are two polarising filters set at angles to each other, with a thin layer of liquid crystal between them. Liquid crystals can flow like a liquid yet line up in order, like a crystal.\n\nA small voltage twists or untwists them, changing how much light gets through the second filter. Each subpixel has its own colour filter and tiny transistor. The weakness is that the backlight stays on behind everything, so dark scenes can look slightly grey rather than truly black."],
    ["OLED: pixels that glow", "OLED pixels make their own light, so black is simply off, though the materials age and can burn in.",
      "OLED stands for organic light-emitting diode. There is no backlight. Each subpixel is a thin sandwich of carbon-based materials that glow when current passes through. To show black, a pixel simply turns off, giving deep blacks and strong contrast, and saving battery on phones when much of the screen is dark. OLED panels can be thin and flexible, which makes folding phones possible.\n\nThe trade-offs are cost and ageing. The organic materials slowly lose brightness, and blue ones fade fastest. An image left on screen for very long periods, such as a news ticker or game scoreboard, can leave a faint permanent ghost called burn-in. Manufacturers fight it with tricks such as subtly shifting the image."],
    ["Frames per second", "A screen shows rapid still frames, and a higher refresh rate makes motion feel smoother.",
      "A screen never shows true motion. It shows a rapid series of still images, like a flipbook. The number of redraws each second is the refresh rate, measured in hertz. Many screens refresh 60 times a second, while newer phones, gaming monitors and televisions reach 120 hertz or more, making scrolling and fast games feel smoother.\n\nFilms are traditionally shot at only 24 frames per second, so a screen often shows each movie frame more than once. Some phones change their refresh rate on the fly, dropping as low as one hertz for a still image to save battery, then jumping back up as soon as you scroll. The faster your finger moves, the more those extra frames matter."],
    ["Resolution and the eye", "Beyond a certain pixel density your eye cannot see extra detail, so more pixels are not always better.",
      "More pixels are not always better. Whether you can see the difference depends on pixel size, screen size and viewing distance. The eye can only resolve detail down to a certain fineness, and once pixels are smaller than that at your distance, adding more brings little visible gain.\n\nApple built its 'Retina' marketing in 2010 around exactly this idea: pixels too small to pick out individually at a typical viewing distance. It also explains why, for most people watching from the couch, an 8K television offers only a modest improvement over 4K. Contrast, brightness, colour accuracy and how cleanly the screen handles fast motion often matter more to how good a picture actually looks."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How does a screen produce yellow?", ["It uses a dedicated yellow subpixel","It turns all three subpixels fully off","It lights red and green subpixels together","It lights blue and green subpixels together"], 2, "Red and green light mixed together stimulate our cones the way yellow does."],
    [1, "Why can dark scenes look grey on an LCD?", ["The liquid crystals make their own grey light","The backlight stays on behind every pixel","The colour filters turn white into grey","LCD panels use fewer pixels in dark areas"], 1, "Filters cannot block all the always-on backlight, so black is rarely fully black."],
    [2, "What causes burn-in on an OLED screen?", ["The backlight overheating over time","Dust trapped between the polarising filters","Using the screen at a high refresh rate","Static images ageing some pixels faster than others"], 3, "Organic materials fade with use, so pixels showing the same image wear differently."],
    [3, "Why might a phone drop its refresh rate to one hertz?", ["To make scrolling feel smoother","To save battery when showing a still image","To match the 24 frames of a film","To reduce burn-in on the display"], 1, "There is no need to redraw an unchanged image, so slowing the refresh saves power."],
    [4, "Why does an 8K television often look only slightly better than 4K from the couch?", ["8K screens use a lower refresh rate","8K panels have fewer colours available","Your eye cannot resolve the extra detail at that distance","The extra pixels are hidden behind the backlight"], 2, "Past the eye's limit at a given distance, extra pixels bring little visible gain."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Everything on your screen is built from just three colours of light. How?"});

WB.L("transistor-story", "technology", "intermediate", "The transistor: the switch that changed everything",
  "In 1947 two physicists built a clumsy contraption of gold foil, plastic and germanium. Its descendants now number in the billions inside your phone.",
  [
    ["The trouble with valves", "Vacuum tubes made early electronics possible but were bulky, hot and unreliable, so Bell Labs sought a solid replacement.",
      "In the first half of the twentieth century, electronics meant vacuum tubes, known in Australia and Britain as valves. These glass bulbs could amplify a signal or switch a current, making radio, television and early computers possible. But they were bulky, hot and power hungry, and their filaments burned out like light globes.\n\nENIAC, an American computer completed in 1945, used around 17,500 of them, filled a large room and drew about 150 kilowatts. Tubes failed regularly, and finding the dead one took time. AT&T, whose network relied on huge numbers of tubes and mechanical switches, wanted something better. Its research arm, Bell Labs, went looking for a solid replacement."],
    ["Christmas at Bell Labs", "In December 1947 Bardeen and Brattain built the first transistor, and Shockley soon designed a sturdier version.",
      "Bell Labs assembled a team to study semiconductors, materials such as germanium and silicon that conduct better than an insulator but worse than a metal. In December 1947, John Bardeen and Walter Brattain pressed two closely spaced gold contacts onto a slab of germanium and found that a small current through one controlled a larger current through the other. The device amplified a signal. They demonstrated it on 23 December. It was awkward, held together with a plastic wedge and a spring, but it worked.\n\nTheir group leader, William Shockley, was reportedly frustrated at not being directly involved. Within weeks he worked out a sturdier design, the junction transistor, which became the basis for practical devices."],
    ["Why a switch matters", "A transistor can amplify and act as a switch with no moving parts, and switches are the foundation of computing.",
      "A transistor does two jobs. It can amplify, turning a weak signal into a stronger copy, which made pocket transistor radios possible. And it can act as a switch with no moving parts: a small voltage on one terminal turns a larger current on or off.\n\nThat second job is the foundation of computing. Computers represent information as on and off, one and zero. A few transistors make a logic gate, a circuit whose output is on only if both inputs are on, or if either is. Combine gates and you can add, compare and store numbers. Because transistors switch billions of times a second using very little power, enormous numbers can work together on a single chip."],
    ["Walkout and the chip", "A walkout from Shockley's lab seeded Silicon Valley, and the integrated circuit put whole circuits on one chip.",
      "Bardeen, Brattain and Shockley shared the 1956 Nobel Prize in Physics. Shockley had by then set up a lab in Mountain View, California, but his suspicious management drove eight young scientists to walk out in 1957. The 'traitorous eight' founded Fairchild Semiconductor, a seedbed for Silicon Valley. Robert Noyce and Gordon Moore later founded Intel in 1968. Bardeen won a second Nobel in 1972 for superconductivity, the only person to win the physics prize twice.\n\nWiring individual transistors by hand became the problem. In 1958 Jack Kilby at Texas Instruments built a circuit in a single piece of germanium. Soon after, Noyce proposed a more practical silicon design using the planar process of Jean Hoerni. Both are credited with the integrated circuit."],
    ["Moore's prediction", "Moore's law is an observation that chip transistor counts roughly double every two years, a trend now slowing.",
      "In 1965 Gordon Moore, then at Fairchild, wrote a magazine article noting that components on a chip had been doubling every year, and predicted this would continue for at least a decade. In 1975 he revised the pace to roughly every two years. This became Moore's law, though it is an observation about engineering and economics, not a law of nature. It held remarkably well for about half a century, partly because the industry treated it as a target.\n\nModern chips contain tens of billions of transistors, each far smaller than a bacterium. Progress has slowed as parts of transistors shrink to just a few atoms thick, and engineers debate how much longer the trend can continue."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did engineers want to replace vacuum tubes?", ["They were too cheap for large companies to trust","They could only handle signals from radio stations","They were bulky, hot and prone to burning out","They needed a new kind of glass that was scarce"], 2, "Tubes were large and power hungry, and frequent failures made big machines like ENIAC hard to keep running."],
    [1, "What did the first transistor do?", ["Stored information as a magnetic pattern","Let a small current control a larger one","Produced light from an electric current","Converted heat directly into electricity"], 1, "One current controlling another is what let it amplify signals and switch."],
    [2, "Why are transistors the foundation of computing?", ["They store data on tiny spinning magnetic discs","They turn digital signals into radio waves","They can only be built from rare precious metals","They can switch on and off without moving parts"], 3, "On and off states represent one and zero, and gates built from them can add, compare and store numbers."],
    [3, "What problem did the integrated circuit solve?", ["The tangle of wiring separate transistors by hand","The shortage of germanium for early transistors","The weak signal from point-contact transistors","The high price of gold for the contacts"], 0, "Making components and connections together on one piece turned electronics into something like printing."],
    [4, "What is Moore's law?", ["An observation that transistor counts double every two years or so","A physical law limiting how small a transistor can be","A rule that chip prices must double every year","A rule that chips should use half the power each year"], 0, "It is an engineering and economic trend, not a law of nature, and it is slowing."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Two physicists built a clumsy gadget in 1947. Now billions sit in your pocket."});

WB.L("phone-cameras", "technology", "intermediate", "How your phone camera takes a photo",
  "A phone camera's lens is smaller than a pea, yet it can rival cameras costing thousands of dollars. The secret is less about glass and more about software.",
  [
    ["Light in buckets", "A camera sensor counts photons in millions of tiny photosites, and software later guesses the missing colours.",
      "A digital camera uses an image sensor, a silicon chip covered in millions of light-sensitive sites called photosites. Each works like a bucket left out in the rain. During an exposure, photons strike the silicon and knock electrons loose, and the photosite collects them. Brighter light means more charge. Afterwards the charge is measured and turned into a number. Almost all phones use CMOS sensors, built like computer chips, which made them cheap and power-efficient.\n\nA photosite counts light but cannot see colour. Most sensors put a red, green or blue filter over each one, in a checkerboard patented by Kodak's Bryce Bayer in 1976, with twice as many green as red or blue. Software estimates the two missing colours at each point from its neighbours, called demosaicing."],
    ["The small-sensor problem", "Small phone sensors catch few photons in dim light, producing noise, and more megapixels can make this worse.",
      "Phones are thin, so their sensors and lenses must be small. A full-frame professional sensor is about 36 by 24 millimetres, and most phone sensors are a fraction of that area. A smaller sensor gathers less light, so in a dim room each photosite may collect only a few photons.\n\nLight arrives randomly, so with few photons the counts fluctuate from one photosite to the next, and the image fills with grainy speckles called noise. The electronics add a little of their own. This is a physical limit, not a manufacturing fault, and explains why phone photos once looked so poor indoors and at night. Cramming in more megapixels can make it worse, because each photosite becomes smaller still."],
    ["Many photos in one", "Computational photography merges several frames into one photo, cancelling noise and keeping detail in light and dark areas.",
      "The breakthrough was computational photography. A modern phone is often already capturing frames before you press the shutter, and combines several into one picture. Averaging many frames cancels much of the random noise, much as averaging many people's guesses cancels individual errors.\n\nCombining shorter and longer exposures keeps detail in both a bright sky and dark shadows, a technique called high dynamic range, or HDR. Night modes capture frames over a second or more and align them to correct for shaky hands. All this happens in moments on dedicated processors. The photo you see is not a single instant of light but a carefully assembled composite."],
    ["Software makes choices", "After merging, the phone edits the picture, so different brands can render the same scene very differently.",
      "After merging frames, the phone keeps editing. It adjusts white balance so a white shirt looks white under orange indoor light, sharpens edges, smooths skin and boosts colours to look pleasing. Many phones recognise what is in the scene, such as food, faces or sky, and treat each part differently.\n\nPortrait mode imitates the soft background blur of a large lens. A tiny lens produces little natural blur, so software estimates how far away each part of the scene is, using two cameras, depth sensors or machine learning, then blurs the background artificially. Look closely at hair or glasses edges and you can sometimes spot mistakes. Different brands make different choices, which is why the same scene can look noticeably different on two phones."],
    ["Still a photograph?", "A phone photo is the camera's best estimate of a scene, and heavy processing can blur capturing and creating.",
      "All cameras interpret the world. Film chemistry favoured some colours over others, and photographers have always chosen exposure and framing. But phones push interpretation much further, and the line between capturing and creating has blurred. Some features can erase unwanted people from the background, or combine the best expressions from several shots of a group.\n\nA well-known controversy arose in 2023 over phone photos of the Moon. A tester showed that one brand's processing added lunar detail missing from a deliberately blurred original, drawing on a model trained on what the Moon looks like. Most processing is harmless and simply produces better pictures. Still, a modern phone photo is best seen as the camera's best estimate of a scene, not a perfect copy."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "How does a phone sensor get colour information?", ["Each photosite measures red, green and blue at once","Filters over photosites, with software estimating the missing colours","The lens separates light into colours before it reaches the sensor","Photosites change colour as brightness increases"], 1, "Each photosite sees one colour, so software fills in the other two from neighbouring points."],
    [1, "Why do small sensors produce noisy photos in dim light?", ["The colour filters add random speckles of their own","Small lenses cannot focus light in dim rooms","Each photosite catches few photons, so chance variation shows","Dim light makes the sensor overheat and misfire"], 2, "With few photons, random fluctuations between photosites appear as grain."],
    [2, "How does combining many frames improve a photo?", ["It averages out noise and keeps light and dark detail","It makes the lens collect more light each frame","It speeds up the shutter so every frame is sharper","It swaps the sensor's colour filters for better ones"], 0, "Random errors cancel when frames are averaged, and mixing exposures handles bright and dark areas."],
    [3, "How does portrait mode create background blur?", ["The tiny lens produces strong blur naturally","Software estimates depth and blurs the background artificially","A second lens physically defocuses the background","The sensor moves back and forth during the shot"], 1, "A small lens gives little natural blur, so software imitates a large lens."],
    [4, "What does the 2023 Moon controversy show about phone photos?", ["The Moon is too bright for any phone sensor","Phones always show the Moon exactly as it is","The Moon looks different on every phone sensor","Processing can add detail the sensor never captured"], 3, "A phone photo is an estimate of a scene, which can include detail added by a trained model."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A lens smaller than a pea rivals pricey cameras. The secret is mostly software."});

WB.L("text-encoding", "technology", "intermediate", "How computers store letters and emoji",
  "Computers only understand numbers. So how does a letter, a Chinese character or a laughing-crying face survive the trip from your phone to someone else's?",
  [
    ["Every letter is a number", "Computers store text as numbers using a shared table, and ASCII was the first widely successful one, though only for English.",
      "Inside a computer there are no letters, only binary numbers. To store text, everyone must agree on a table mapping each character to a number. Send 65 and a receiving computer using the same table shows a capital A. Early computers each had their own tables, so text from one machine could turn to gibberish on another.\n\nIn 1963 American standards bodies published the first version of ASCII, the American Standard Code for Information Interchange, refined over the following years. It used 7 bits, allowing 128 codes: English letters in upper and lower case, digits, punctuation and control codes, such as a 'bell' code that rang a bell on teleprinters. ASCII was a huge success, but it was designed for English and left out most of the world's writing."],
    ["Babel on the screen", "Competing extensions of ASCII caused garbled text, known in Japan as mojibake, whenever the wrong table was used.",
      "Computers usually store data in bytes of eight bits, which hold 256 values, so many systems used the spare 128 slots for accented letters, symbols or other alphabets. But different countries and companies filled the slots differently. Greek, Russian, Hebrew and Western European text each had their own versions, and Chinese and Japanese, with thousands of characters, needed more complex schemes.\n\nOpen a document using the wrong table and the numbers came out as the wrong characters. Japanese users had a word for the jumble: mojibake, roughly 'character transformation'. Anyone who has seen an apostrophe turn into a string like â€™ on a web page has met the same problem."],
    ["One code for all", "Unicode gives every character in every writing system a unique number, keeping ASCII's values for compatibility.",
      "In the late 1980s, engineers including Joe Becker of Xerox and Lee Collins and Mark Davis of Apple began working on a universal solution. The Unicode Consortium was founded in 1991 and published the first version of the standard that year. Its goal: one number, called a code point, for every character in every writing system, past and present.\n\nCapital A is still 65, for compatibility with ASCII, and is written in Unicode as U+0041. Unicode now covers well over 150,000 characters across more than 160 scripts, including Arabic, Devanagari, Cherokee, Egyptian hieroglyphs and cuneiform. New characters are added through a formal proposal process, and the standard is updated roughly once a year."],
    ["A design on a placemat", "UTF-8 stores Unicode in one to four bytes while leaving plain English text identical to ASCII.",
      "Giving characters numbers is only half the job; you also need a way to store them as bytes. One evening in September 1992, computer scientists Ken Thompson and Rob Pike, then at Bell Labs, sketched a scheme on a placemat in a New Jersey diner. It became UTF-8.\n\nIts elegance is that ordinary English text is stored exactly as in ASCII, one byte per character, so old files and programs keep working. Other characters use two, three or four bytes, with bit patterns that clearly mark where each character starts, so software never mistakes part of one character for another. UTF-8 spread steadily, and today the great majority of web pages use it, a quiet but enormous victory for compatibility."],
    ["Emoji arrive", "Emoji are Unicode characters whose artwork varies by platform, and many are built by joining several code points.",
      "Emoji come from Japan: e means picture and moji means character. The resemblance to 'emotion' is a coincidence. In 1999 Shigetaka Kurita created 176 simple 12 by 12 pixel designs for NTT Docomo's mobile internet service, now held by New York's Museum of Modern Art, though another carrier had offered picture symbols slightly earlier. Each carrier used its own codes, so emoji broke between networks. Pressure from companies including Google and Apple led to emoji joining Unicode in 2010.\n\nUnicode gives each emoji a code point and name, such as U+1F602, 'face with tears of joy', but not the artwork, so Apple, Google and others draw their own. Skin tones attach as modifiers, and family emoji join several with an invisible zero-width joiner, so one picture can use seven or more code points."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did early text often turn to gibberish between computers?", ["Letters cannot be stored in binary","Text was too long to be sent","Each machine used its own table of character numbers","The first computers could only show numbers"], 2, "Without a shared table, the same number could mean different characters on different machines."],
    [1, "What causes mojibake?", ["Opening text with the wrong character table","Using too many emoji in one message","A damaged hard drive","Sending text across time zones"], 0, "The numbers are correct but get interpreted through the wrong table, giving the wrong characters."],
    [2, "What is Unicode's main goal?", ["A faster way to type English letters","One number for every character in every writing system","A smaller set of characters for the web","A separate code for each country"], 1, "A single universal code point per character ends the clash between competing tables."],
    [3, "Why was UTF-8's design so useful?", ["It uses exactly two bytes for every character","It removes the need for Unicode","It only supports emoji and symbols","It stores plain English text exactly like ASCII"], 3, "Compatibility with ASCII let old files and programs keep working."],
    [4, "Why can the same emoji look different on two phones?", ["Unicode sets the code point and name, not the artwork","Each phone stores emoji as small photographs","Phone makers assign their own numbers to each emoji","Screens redraw emoji differently each time they load"], 0, "Each company draws its own designs for the same Unicode code point."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"Computers only understand numbers. So how does a laughing face reach your friend's phone?"});

WB.L("open-source", "technology", "intermediate", "Open source: the free code running the world",
  "Much of the digital world runs on software written in the open and given away. In 2024, one engineer's curiosity about a half-second delay showed both the strength and the fragility of that system.",
  [
    ["Recipes anyone can read", "Open source software publishes its source code so anyone can study, change and share it under a licence.",
      "Software begins as source code, instructions written in a programming language. Most commercial programs are distributed only in compiled form, which computers can run but humans can barely read, so the recipe stays secret. Open source software publishes the recipe. Anyone can study it, fix it, improve it or build on it, under a licence that sets the rules. Some licences let you do almost anything; others require you to share your changes too.\n\nOpen source does not mean nobody is paid. Many developers are employed by companies that rely on the software. But the code itself is shared, and it is everywhere: phones, cars, televisions, routers and the servers behind most websites."],
    ["From printer to Linux", "Frustration with locked-up software led Stallman to start free software, and Torvalds' hobby kernel completed it.",
      "By the late 1970s software was increasingly locked up as a commercial product. Richard Stallman, an MIT programmer, later described a formative frustration: a jamming laser printer he could not fix because the maker would not share the source code. In 1983 he announced the GNU project to build a complete operating system of free software, and in 1985 founded the Free Software Foundation. 'Free' meant freedom, not price. His GNU General Public License required shared changes to stay free, a twist nicknamed copyleft.\n\nGNU still lacked a kernel. In August 1991 Linus Torvalds, a 21-year-old Helsinki student, posted that he was writing a free operating system, just a hobby, not big and professional like GNU. Programmers worldwide sent improvements, and Linux was born. He later created Git too."],
    ["Running the world", "Linux and other open source projects underpin phones, servers, supercomputers and online security.",
      "Linux is far more widespread than most people realise. Android, the world's most popular phone system, is built on the Linux kernel. Linux runs most of the servers behind websites and cloud services, and since 2017 every one of the 500 fastest supercomputers on the TOP500 list has run it. Open source also supplies browser engines, databases, programming languages and the encryption libraries protecting online banking.\n\nCompanies build on this shared foundation rather than writing everything from scratch. Even firms that once feared open source came around. Microsoft's chief executive Steve Ballmer called Linux 'a cancer' in 2001; Microsoft later became one of open source's biggest contributors."],
    ["Holding up the internet", "Widely used open source code can depend on a few underfunded volunteers, which makes serious flaws a shared risk.",
      "Shared code has a weakness: everyone uses it, but no one in particular is responsible for it. In 2014 researchers discovered Heartbleed, a serious flaw in OpenSSL, encryption software used by a large share of secure web servers. The project was run by a handful of people, with only one working on it full time, and received only around 2000 US dollars a year in donations.\n\nIn 2021 another severe flaw, Log4Shell, turned up in Log4j, a widely used logging tool maintained largely by volunteers. Each crisis pushed companies and governments to fund critical projects more seriously. A popular web cartoon sums up the worry: digital infrastructure balanced on one small project that a lone volunteer has thanklessly maintained for years."],
    ["Half a second", "A tiny delay noticed by one engineer exposed a backdoor planted in xz Utils after two years of trust-building.",
      "In March 2024 Andres Freund, a Microsoft engineer who works on the PostgreSQL database, noticed something odd: logging in to a test machine used more processor time than it should, adding about half a second. Curious, he traced the delay to xz Utils, a small compression tool in many Linux systems. Someone had hidden a backdoor in it that could have let an attacker take control of affected servers.\n\nThe culprit, using the name Jia Tan, had spent around two years earning the trust of the project's overstretched volunteer maintainer before gaining the access to slip it in. It was caught before reaching the stable versions of major Linux distributions. Who was behind it remains unknown, though many security experts suspect a well-resourced, possibly state-backed group."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What makes software open source?", ["Its source code is public to study and change","It is always free of charge to the user","It is written only by volunteers","It can only run on Linux"], 0, "Open source means the recipe is shared under a licence, not that nobody gets paid."],
    [1, "What did Stallman mean by 'free' software?", ["It never costs the user any money","It can never be sold by anyone","Users are free to study and change it","It comes with no licence or rules"], 2, "The point was freedom, free as in free speech, not free as in free beer."],
    [2, "Where is Linux commonly used today?", ["Only on home desktop computers and laptops","Only in specialist research laboratories","Almost nowhere outside hobbyist computers","Android phones, web servers and supercomputers"], 3, "Linux quietly powers much of the digital world, from phones to supercomputers."],
    [3, "What did Heartbleed reveal about open source?", ["Vital shared code can rely on a few underfunded people","Open source is always less secure than commercial code","Encryption software cannot be fixed","Volunteers refuse to maintain code"], 0, "Everyone relies on the code, yet few fund or maintain it, so flaws carry shared risk."],
    [4, "How was the xz Utils backdoor discovered?", ["A routine government audit","A security alarm at a major bank","An engineer chasing a half-second login delay","A public report from the attacker"], 2, "Careful curiosity about a small slowdown uncovered a hidden attack."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"A half-second delay on one test machine uncovered a plot against the internet."});

WB.L("halting-problem", "technology", "advanced", "Turing and the problems no computer can solve",
  "Before electronic computers existed, a 23-year-old mathematician proved there are questions no computer, however powerful, will ever be able to answer.",
  [
    ["Hilbert's challenge", "Turing answered Hilbert's decision problem by first defining exactly what a mechanical procedure is.",
      "In 1928 David Hilbert and Wilhelm Ackermann posed the Entscheidungsproblem, or ‘decision problem’. Could a definite step-by-step procedure decide whether any statement in formal logic is provable? If so, mathematics could in principle be mechanised. In 1931 Kurt Gödel had shown that any consistent system rich enough for arithmetic contains true statements it cannot prove, but the decision problem was a separate question. Answering it needed a precise definition of ‘mechanical procedure’. In 1936 Alan Turing, a 23-year-old fellow of King's College, Cambridge, supplied one. He imagined a simple machine: an endless tape of squares, a head that reads, writes or erases one symbol at a time and moves left or right, and a finite table of rules. Anything that can be calculated by a precise procedure, he argued, such a machine can calculate."],
    ["One machine for all", "A universal machine can run any other machine's rules, which is the idea behind every modern computer.",
      "Turing's paper, ‘On Computable Numbers’, held a second startling idea. The rule table of any Turing machine can itself be written down as symbols on a tape. So a universal machine, given a description of any other machine plus its input, can simulate it step by step and produce the same result. One machine can do the work of every machine, if it is handed the right instructions. That is exactly what a modern computer is. Your laptop needs no different hardware to be a word processor, a game or a calculator; it simply loads a different program. Historians debate how directly Turing's abstract idea influenced the engineers who built the first stored-program computers in the late 1940s, but the concept existed in 1936."],
    ["The program that never stops", "The halting problem asks whether a perfect checker could tell, in advance, if any program will finish or run forever.",
      "Anyone who has watched a frozen app knows that programs can get stuck in endless loops. A perfect checker, able to examine any program and its input and say in advance whether it will stop or run forever, would be enormously useful. Some cases are easy: a program that counts from one to ten halts, while ‘repeat forever’ does not. Others are fiendish. Imagine a program that searches for an even number greater than 2 that is not the sum of two primes, and stops only if it finds one. Whether it ever halts depends on Goldbach's conjecture, unsolved since 1742. A perfect halting checker would settle such questions instantly. This is the halting problem."],
    ["A proof by contradiction", "No general halting checker can exist, because any such checker could be made to contradict itself.",
      "Suppose someone claims to have built a perfect checker, called H. In the form usually taught today, the argument runs like this. Build a program called Contrary that takes any program, asks H whether it halts when given its own code, and then does the opposite. If H says it halts, Contrary loops forever; if H says it loops, Contrary stops. Now feed Contrary its own code. Whatever H predicts, Contrary does the reverse, so H is wrong. No such H can exist. Turing used reasoning of this kind to answer Hilbert: no general mechanical procedure can decide every mathematical question. The American logician Alonzo Church reached the same conclusion independently, by a different route, shortly before."],
    ["Living with the impossible", "Because some questions are undecidable, no virus scanner or bug checker can ever be perfect.",
      "The halting problem is no quirk of old mathematics. A later result, Rice's theorem, extends it: no program can perfectly decide, for every possible program, any meaningful property of how that program behaves, such as whether it will ever leak a password. That is why no antivirus software can be guaranteed to catch every piece of malicious code, and why tools that check programs for bugs must accept limits. They can miss problems, raise false alarms or give up on some cases. In practice, engineers work around this with time limits, careful restrictions and checkers that are right most of the time. Turing's 1936 insight remains a humbling boundary. Some questions are not merely hard for computers. They are impossible."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Turing need to describe his imaginary machine to answer Hilbert?", ["Computers were already built and needed testing","Hilbert's question needed ‘mechanical procedure’ defined first","Gödel had already answered the decision problem","He planned to build it from vacuum tubes"], 1, "To prove what no procedure can do, Turing first had to define exactly what a procedure is."],
    [1, "What makes a universal machine the idea behind modern computers?", ["It is faster than any machine built for one task","It stores all its data on an endless tape","One machine can do any job if given instructions","It keeps running without ever being switched off"], 2, "A computer needs no new hardware for each job, only a different program."],
    [2, "Why is a program that hunts for a Goldbach counterexample a hard test for a halting checker?", ["It always runs for exactly one second","It needs more memory than any computer has","It is too short for a checker to analyse","Whether it halts hinges on an unsolved maths problem"], 3, "A perfect checker would have to settle open mathematical questions, which hints at how demanding the task is."],
    [3, "What does the Contrary argument show?", ["A perfect halting checker would be wrong about Contrary","Loops should never be used in programs","Computers reason more slowly than people do","A halting checker is possible but very slow"], 0, "Whatever H predicts about Contrary, Contrary does the opposite, so no perfect H can exist."],
    [4, "What practical consequence follows from undecidability?", ["Computers cannot reliably add very large numbers","No antivirus can be guaranteed to catch all malware","Programs cannot be copied without errors","Internet speeds have a fixed upper limit"], 1, "Perfectly judging what any program will do is impossible, so checkers must accept limits."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-10-26","push":"In 1936, before computers existed, a young mathematician proved what they could never do."});

